# Audit Rail — UI container environment.
# Copy to ui.env. Read at CONTAINER START, so changing it in Portainer and restarting is
# enough — nothing is baked into the JavaScript bundle.

# ── API_URL — optional, and for your setup it should stay EMPTY ─────────────────────────
#
# You said your own nginx routes /api straight to the API container. That means the browser
# already sees one origin, the SPA's relative `/api` calls land correctly, and this container
# needs to forward nothing. Leave it unset.
#
# Set it only if you would rather send the WHOLE domain to this container and have it forward
# /api onward — one upstream in your nginx instead of two. Then:
#
#   API_URL=http://audit-rail-api:5007        (same docker network)
#   API_URL=http://10.0.0.12:5007             (different host)
#
# NO trailing slash and NO path: nginx must forward the URI unchanged, because the FastAPI
# routers are mounted at /api. The entrypoint strips a trailing slash for you and logs the
# upstream it settled on, so `docker logs audit-rail-ui` tells you which mode you are in.
#
# What this must never become is a DIFFERENT origin baked into the frontend. The public
# signing page fetches /api/sign/... with a hardcoded root-relative path, its policy images
# are plain <img src="/api/sign/…"> tags that cannot carry a bearer token, and stored document
# HTML contains literal /api/documents/images/<uuid> srcs that are hashed into
# content_sha256 and so cannot be rewritten. One origin, at the edge, keeps all three true.
#
# API_URL=
